A||N||5||general||Temp guage/coolant sensor||||5||
Z||000000||general||04-27-2000||01:03 AM||||    I have been dealing with a problem pertaining to my temperature guage coolant sensor/sending unit.After checking the wiring to my temp guage it seems that my problem is in my sending unit.I have replaced the sending unit once already and am preparing to do it again.I once saw a reply on this forum where theogre posted a listing of specs on sensors under the heading HOW TO:MAT & COOLANT SENSORS ,It stated that the coolant sensor should have about 1800 ohms resistance at 100 deg.F and about 185 ohms resistance at 210 deg.F . My question is should the measurement be taken across the 2 terminals or should this be done with the sensor installed and the ohmeter connected at some other point in the wiring with the ignition on? Any thoughts ???||||reg||5
Z||000001||Phil||04-27-2000||07:06 AM||||Make sure you are looking at the right sensor. On the V6 the gage/light sensor is in the head just below the dist. Do not use teflon tape on the sensor for the shell must be grounded. Resistance readings should be taken from the pin to ground (shell). One pin should be open to ground (idiot light) and the other  1356 ohms@100 F and 55@260 deg  disconnect the wires before measuring||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||04-27-2000||12:11 PM||||That how to is ONLY for ECM sensors. The table is not valid for the coolant gauge/alert.<P>As phil said, DO NOT use teflon tape one the gauge/alert sender. Don't use tape on the oil pressure sender or the fan swtich ether. All 3 of those use the sendor shell for a ground path. Best thing to use on these is a <B>little</B> pipe sealant. infact if you'r haveing problems with any of these take them out, clean the threads, and reinstall them. <P>The screw in sensors are all "NPT." that means they use a tapered thread. Don't use bolt taps to clean out the holes.<P><BR>All the ECM sensors, with the exception of O2, are isolated from the shell. You can use tape or anything else that you like to seal the threaded ones.||||reg||1
Z||000003||general||05-22-2000||08:14 PM||||    Thanks for the tips guys!I have tried these suggestions and have finally found a sensor that I can get a reading off of.The problem that I have now is that I still can't get a reading to show up on my guage.I replaced the sensor and even soldered on another plug at the sensor to ensure a good connection.From what I understand the guage can only be replaced as a unit along with the rest of the cluster . Is it possible to just replace this with an aftermarket guage  ,and if so ,will this cause any problems with the ECM ? Any thoughts would be appreciated .   general||||reg||9
Z||000004||beachbomb||05-22-2000||09:11 PM||||HI, if you are trying to get your temp gauge to work, first off, if you have a 4 cylinder engine, i recently had this same problem, gauge would not read and temps, i replaced the temp sense gauge. after trying 3 different new switches i was told the i needed a collant temp SENSOR, not a coolant temp swicth(damn parts look identical!) so i bought a SENSOR, put it in the top part of the termostat housing and it worked, maybe thats the prob, e-mail me for any questions..jim||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jay||05-23-2000||05:28 PM||||You don't have to change the entire cluster to change the temp gauge.  I had a similar problem and the problem was the gauge. Simply take out the entire cluster.  Carefully open it up and remove the gauge.  I replaced mine with one from a scrap yard and it has worked fine ever since.  Just be sure to take the cluster out properly, or else you can break it.||||reg||1
